course: myths
lesson: 19
title: Kings, queens and emperors
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ruby is sure Napoleon was tiny and bossy because of it, Henry swears Nero fiddled while Rome burned, and the quote card on the wall says Marie Antoinette told hungry people to "eat cake".
explain: Famous rulers collect stories the way a celebrity collects gossip: enemies drew rude cartoons, writers made up neat quotes, and painters and films added the rest hundreds of years later.
explain: Historians work like detectives: they ask who first told the story, how long after the event, and whether that person was there or had a reason to make the ruler look bad.
explain: Napoleon was about average height, the "cake" line has no evidence she said it, and Cleopatra's family was Greek; the true stories are often stranger and more interesting than the myths.
explain: When a friend repeats a royal legend, don't laugh at them; say "I heard that too, but I read it might have been made up by his enemies", and look it up together.
fact: Napoleon was about 1.69 metres tall, an ordinary or even slightly tall height for a Frenchman of his time.
fact: The "tiny Napoleon" idea grew from British cartoons that mocked him, from French inches being longer than English ones, and from his nickname "the little corporal" (a corporal is a low army rank), which was a friendly name, not a measurement.
fact: There is no evidence that Marie Antoinette said "Let them eat cake"; a similar line about a great princess appears in a book by Rousseau written when she was a child of about ten living in Austria.
fact: Nero could not have played the fiddle while Rome burned in the year 64, because the fiddle had not been invented; the Roman historian Tacitus says he was away at Antium, came back and opened shelters, though rumours said he sang about the fall of Troy.
fact: Cleopatra VII was a queen of Egypt from the Ptolemy family, whose family came from Macedonia in Greece; she was the first of her family known to learn the Egyptian language.
fact: Cleopatra lived closer in time to the first Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid of Giza.
fact: Henry VIII was tall, strong and sporty as a young king; he grew very heavy later, after a bad jousting accident in 1536 (jousting is knights on horses charging at each other with long poles) and years of a painful leg wound.
fact: George Washington's false teeth were made from human teeth, animal teeth, ivory and metal, not wood; Elizabeth I's teeth were said to be blackened, probably from sugar.
fact: Kings and queens could not do whatever they liked: they depended on nobles, money, armies, the church and laws such as Magna Carta of 1215, and some rulers were overthrown.
fact: Julius Caesar was made dictator of Rome (a leader given total power for a time) but was never emperor; his adopted son Augustus became the first Roman emperor in 27 BC.
fact: Lady Godiva was a real noblewoman of the 1000s, but the story of her famous ride was first written down more than a hundred years after she died, and "Peeping Tom" was added centuries later still.
fact: Queen Victoria looks stern in photographs partly because people were expected to look serious in photos then; her diaries and letters show she laughed and enjoyed jokes, and a photo of her smiling exists.
fact: A story told by a ruler's enemies, or written down long after the event, is weak evidence and should be checked.
exercise: Ruby says Napoleon was super tiny. How tall was he really? | About 1.69 metres, ordinary or slightly tall for his time | About 1.20 metres, much shorter than his soldiers | About 2 metres, a giant among his generals | Nobody wrote down his height, so we cannot know
exercise: Where did the "tiny Napoleon" idea mostly come from? | British cartoons, longer French inches and a friendly nickname | His own diary, where he often complained about being so very short | The French army, which only ever took very short men as its soldiers | His doctors, who measured him every single year and wrote it all down
exercise: Did Marie Antoinette say "Let them eat cake"? | There is no evidence; a similar line was written when she was a child | Yes, she shouted it from a palace balcony to a crowd | Yes, and she wrote it in a letter to her mother | No, it was her husband the king who said it, standing at the palace gate
exercise: Why couldn't Nero have played the fiddle while Rome burned? | The fiddle had not been invented yet | Nero had broken both his arms that week | Fiddles were banned in Rome during fires | He was too busy fighting a war in Britain
exercise: What does the Roman historian Tacitus say Nero did during the fire? | He was away, came back and opened shelters | He watched happily from the top of his palace | He started the fire himself with a torch | He sailed away and never came back to Rome
exercise: What was Cleopatra's family background? | The Ptolemies, a Greek family from Macedonia | The pharaohs who built the Great Pyramid | A family of Roman generals | An old Egyptian family of farmers from the Nile
exercise: Which is true about Cleopatra and time? | She lived closer to the Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid | She helped to build the Great Pyramid as a young princess | She lived at the same time as the first pharaohs | She lived just a few years after the pyramids were finished
exercise: Ben says Henry VIII was always fat. What is closer to the truth? | He was tall and sporty when young and grew heavy later | He was fat from the day he was born | He was always thin, but he wore thick padded clothes to look grand | He was fat as a boy and very slim as an old man
exercise: What were George Washington's false teeth made of? | Human and animal teeth, ivory and metal | Carved wood from an old oak tree in his garden | Solid gold and nothing else, made by a jeweller | Painted stones picked up from a river bed near home
exercise: Charlie says kings and queens could do anything they liked. What is true? | They depended on nobles, money, armies and laws, and some were overthrown | They could do anything at all, and no one could ever stop them | They had no power whatsoever and only waved at crowds | They could only rule if every person in the land voted for them
exercise: Who was the first Roman emperor? | Augustus | Julius Caesar | Nero | Romulus
exercise: Why should we doubt the story of Lady Godiva's ride? | It was first written down over a hundred years after she died | She never existed, so there was no one to ride | A law of the time said that no rider was ever allowed to cross Coventry | Her horse is known to have been too old to ride
exercise: Why does Queen Victoria look stern in old photos? | People were expected to look serious in photographs then | She was never able to smile because of an illness | She had banned all laughter across the empire after her husband died | Cameras of her time could not show a smile at all
exercise: A story about a ruler was told by his enemies a hundred years later. How strong is that evidence? | Weak; it should be checked against other sources | Very strong, because enemies watch rulers closely | Perfect, because it is very old | Useless, so the ruler must have done the opposite
exercise: What does a good historian ask first about a royal story? | Who told it first, and when, and were they there? | How many people today believe that it is really true? | Does it make a good exciting scene for a big film? | Is it written down somewhere in a big and heavy old book?
test: "napoleon was a short angry midget lol everyone knows" What's the best reply? | He was about average height; British cartoons made him look tiny | You're right, he was the shortest grown man in the whole of France | He was short only when he sat on a horse | Nobody knows, so you can say whatever you like
test: A film shows Nero playing a violin while Rome burns. What is wrong with the scene? | Violins did not exist then, and Tacitus says he was away | Nothing, the film is exactly like the history books say it happened | It should be a guitar, which the Romans loved | He should be playing it in a different part of Rome
test: Your teacher's quote card says "Marie Antoinette: Let them eat cake!" How could you raise it kindly? | "Miss, I read there's no evidence she said it. Could we check?" | "That's rubbish, Miss, everybody knows it's fake!" | Rip the card off the wall at break time | Say nothing, because quote cards are always right
test: Gemma says Cleopatra was "Egyptian like the pyramid pharaohs". What would you add? | She ruled Egypt, but her family was Greek and came much later | She was Roman and never set foot in Egypt | She built the Great Pyramid for her tomb | She was the very first pharaoh of all, long before the pyramids were built
test: "Henry VIII was a fat slob his whole life, look at the pictures" What do the pictures miss? | As a young king he was tall, strong and good at sport | The painters made him look thinner than he really was | He was fat because he was ill from birth | The pictures are of a different king called Henry
test: Maisie mixes up two stories about wooden teeth. Which is true? | Neither had wooden teeth; Washington's were teeth, ivory and metal | Elizabeth I had wooden teeth carved from oak | Washington had wooden teeth painted white | Both of them had wooden teeth made by the same man
test: In a video game, a king orders anything he wants and nobody can say no. What would a real king have needed? | The support of nobles, money, armies and the law | Only a big golden crown and a very loud commanding voice | Nothing at all, because kings were above every single rule | A vote of all the children in the kingdom, held every spring
test: Felix's poster says "Julius Caesar, first emperor of Rome". What should it say? | Julius Caesar, dictator of Rome; Augustus was the first emperor | Julius Caesar, first king of Rome | Julius Caesar, first emperor of Egypt | It is right, so it should not change
test: "Victoria never smiled once, my nan said" How could you check? | Read her diaries and letters, which show her laughing | Count how many smiles there are in her painted portraits | Ask which colour dress she wore most often in photographs | It cannot be checked at all, so just believe what your nan says
test: Amira tells the Lady Godiva story as fact. What is the wise thing to say? | It's a legend written long after; Peeping Tom was added later | Every word is true because it's in a famous painting | She never lived, so the whole story is a lie | Only the part about the horse is a legend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the stature of Napoleon Bonaparte? | He was of approximately average height for a Frenchman of his era | He was of exceptionally diminutive stature by the standards of any era | He exceeded two metres in height | His height was never recorded by any contemporary
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the account of Nero playing the fiddle during the conflagration of Rome cannot be accurate. | The fiddle had not been invented at that time | Nero was demonstrably incapable of music | The fire occurred during the reign of another emperor | Musical performance was prohibited during fires by law
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the first emperor of Rome. | Augustus became the first Roman emperor in 27 BC | Julius Caesar was crowned the first Roman emperor | Nero was the first to hold the imperial title | Cleopatra conferred the imperial title upon Caesar
exam: It is given that an anecdote concerning a monarch was first recorded by his adversaries a century after his death. Determine the appropriate evaluation of such an anecdote. | It constitutes weak evidence and requires corroboration | It constitutes conclusive proof by reason of its antiquity | It must be presumed true unless disproved | It must be presumed to state the opposite of the truth
